<sn><md>### NIC 

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Network Interface Card
A **NIC** is a hardware component that allows a computer or other device to connect to a network.

**Functions:**

* Connects a device to a network.
* Converts computer `data into signals` that can travel through the network medium.
* Receives network signals and converts them back into data.
  ```
  data ⇄ signal
  ```
* Provides a `MAC address` for network communication.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0736/7566/9719/files/SEOon_NICs_Basic_Knowledge.jpg?v=1758523240)
:::
:::

**Types:**
* Ethernet NIC — wired connection
* Wireless NIC — Wi-Fi connection

**Example:** The Ethernet port or Wi-Fi adapter in a laptop is associated with its NIC.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Hub

A **hub** is a basic networking device used to `connect multiple devices in a local network`.

When a hub receives data, it `broadcasts the data to every connected device`.The devices check the destination information and determine whether the data is intended for them.


:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-7
**Characteristics:**

* Works mainly at `OSI Layer 1` — Physical Layer.
* Does not intelligently `!determine the destination` device.
* Creates more `unnecessary network traffic`.
* Generally operates using a `shared communication medium`.
* Mostly `obsolete` in modern networks.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-5
![img](https://images.ctfassets.net/aoyx73g9h2pg/2BeY1nsgq7F1zV7TZezCeV/a01a704bbe01389eebedccdfe285801b/What-is-a-Computer-Network-Hub-Diagram.jpg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Switch

A **switch** `connects multiple devices` within a LAN and forwards data to the [specific destination device]{.fg-teal}.

Unlike a hub, a switch learns the **MAC addresses** of connected devices and uses its MAC address table to decide where to forward frames.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/switch-diagram.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**Characteristics:**

* Mainly operates at `OSI Layer 2` — Data Link Layer.
* Uses **MAC addresses**.
* `Reduces unnecessary traffic` compared with a hub.
* Each switch port can provide a `separate collision domain`.
* Modern Ethernet switches are commonly used in LANs.
:::
:::
</md></sn>